   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to directing the commissioner of health to produce a report on the best practices for the management and treatment of mild traumatic brain injuries in children and adolescents",
    "basePrintNo": "S7004",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7004-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 6,
       "fullName": "Kemp Hannon",
       "imgName": "424_kemp_hannon.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 424,
       "sessionMemberId": 180,
       "sessionYear": 2013,
       "shortName": "HANNON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §206, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7004\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\ndirecting the commissioner of health to produce a report on the best\npractices for the management and treatment of mild traumatic brain\ninjuries in children and adolescents\n\nPurpose:\n\nDirects the commissioner of health to produce and periodically update\na best practices model for the management and treatment of mild\ntraumatic brain injuries.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 amends Section 206 of the public health law by adding a new\nsubdivision 29, directing the commissioner of health to produce a\nreport on the best practices for the management and treatment of mild\ntraumatic brain injuries in children and adolescents and publish this\ninformation on the official health department website.\n\nSection 2 provides an immediate effective date.\n\nJustification:\n\nMild traumatic brain injuries present a serious health threat to\nchildren and adolescents, particularly those participating in\ninter-scholastic athletics. Those who suffer a concussion can have\nsymptoms that include nausea, memory loss, and cognitive impairment,\nwith some cases even resulting in death.\n\nThe Concussion Management and Awareness Act, passed in 2011, provided\ncomprehensive statutory guidance for the management of students\nsuspected of suffering a mild traumatic brain injury during athletic\nplay. This legislation builds on the important guidelines set forth in\nthe Concussion Management Awareness Act, by ensuring that there is\nup-to-date information distributed to providers and the public at\nlarge.\n\nCurrent research regarding concussions has been significant, including\ndedicated programs at SUNY Upstate, the University at Buffalo, the\nUniversity of Rochester Medical Center, and the Brain Trauma\nFoundation. Given the progress to date in the field, the medical\ncommunity as well as the public would benefit from up-to-date\ninformation regarding research into the diagnosis and treatment of\nconcussions. As such, this bill requires that the Commissioner of\nHealth compile information regarding best practices for the treatment\nand diagnosis of concussions to be compiled by the Department of\nHealth and posted on their website.\n\nAs we continue to see the detrimental effects that concussions have on\nour children, it is important that we work to enhance our current\nknowledge about this important public health issue.\n\nLegislative History:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n\nFiscal Implications:\n\nNone.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
